        <div id="quick-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: What Size Generator a Well Pump Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            When the power goes out on a property with a private well, the water goes out with it. No pump means no faucets, no flushing, and no water for livestock or a garden. That makes the well pump the first load most rural homeowners want to back up, and also the most demanding one, because a pump motor draws several times its running current for the split second it starts. Buy a generator sized for the running watts alone and it will stall, trip its breaker or never get the pump spinning.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a second trap: voltage. Most submersible well pumps in the U.S. run on 240 volts, and a large share of popular portable generators, especially quiet inverter models, only produce 120 volts. A generator with a 120/240V locking outlet, connected through a transfer switch or interlock, is what lets the pump run at all. Our general <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a> covers whole-house sizing; this one starts from the pump.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">For a typical 1/2 to 1 HP, 240V submersible pump, a 7,500-10,000-watt dual fuel generator with a 120/240V outlet is the sweet spot.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Larger pumps, or pumps that must start while the house is running, need 10,000+ starting watts. Battery options need native 240V output and enough surge.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The seven picks below include five gas and dual fuel generators and two large power stations that produce 240 volts. They are described from manufacturers' published specifications, not hands-on testing, and the sizing numbers in this guide are planning ranges. The authoritative number is your pump motor maker's generator sizing table, which you can match to the horsepower and voltage printed on the pump's control box or the well's paperwork.
          </p>

          <p>
            Throughout, one safety rule applies: a generator connects to house wiring only through a transfer switch or a properly installed interlock kit with a power inlet, done by a licensed electrician. A well pump circuit is a 240-volt, double-pole circuit, and wiring it to a generator is not a do-it-yourself job. Generators run outdoors only, far from doors and windows, with working carbon monoxide alarms inside.
          </p>
        </div>

