                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3/4 HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,400-1,600W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000-4,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6-8 A</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1 HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,800-2,200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4,000-6,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8-10 A</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.5 HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,400-3,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6,000-7,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 10-13 A</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            These are broad planning ranges compiled from typical published pump and generator sizing charts, not specs for your motor. Two pumps of the same horsepower can differ meaningfully depending on the brand, depth, whether the motor is 2-wire or 3-wire, and the service factor. The nameplate on the motor or the label on the control box gives the real full-load and service-factor amps; multiply the running amps by the voltage for a rough running-watt figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Submersible motor makers also publish generator sizing tables, and those are the numbers to trust. They typically recommend generators rated well above the pump's running watts, because a generator's voltage sags under a motor start, and a heavily loaded generator can sag far enough to stall the motor. Many of these tables call for a generator in the range of four to five kilowatts or more for a 1 HP pump, and larger again for externally regulated versus internally regulated generators. If your pump maker's table calls for a certain size, buy at least that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Well pump amp draw also matters for the breaker and wiring. A 240V pump circuit is typically on a double-pole breaker sized by the pump installer, and the starting current is high enough that undersized generators often trip their own output breaker rather than stall. If your generator's 240V outlet trips every time the pump kicks on, the generator is too small for that pump, or other motors are starting at the same moment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="voltage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 120V vs 240V Pumps and the 240V Power Outlet You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before comparing generators, find out what voltage your pump uses. Deep submersible pumps, which sit down in the well casing, are most often 240 volts. Shallow-well jet pumps, which sit above ground in a basement or pump house, are often dual-voltage and may be wired for either 120V or 240V. The main panel tells you a lot: a well pump on a double-pole breaker, the kind that spans two slots, is a 240V pump. A single-pole breaker means 120V.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 240V pump needs a generator that produces 240 volts, which in practice means a generator with a 120/240V 30-amp locking outlet, labeled L14-30R, or a 50-amp 14-50R outlet. Every gas generator on this list has one. Many popular small and quiet inverter generators do not; they produce 120 volts only and cannot run a 240V pump at any wattage. That is why small and mid-size units like those in our <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">small generator guide</a> are often the wrong tool for a well.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE 240V OUTLET</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">That L14-30R is not a plug for your pump. It feeds a power inlet and transfer switch.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The generator's 240V power outlet connects with a heavy-duty cord to an inlet box on the house, which feeds a transfer switch or interlocked panel. Your pump stays wired to its own circuit; the transfer equipment decides whether that circuit gets grid or generator power.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If your pump is 120V, your options open up: any generator with enough starting watts can run it through a transfer switch, including some 120V-only inverter units, though you still need the surge headroom. A 120V pump draws roughly twice the amps of the same-horsepower 240V pump, so check that the generator's 120V outlets and the transfer switch circuit are rated for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery systems face the same voltage question. Most portable power stations produce only 120V and cannot run a 240V pump. The Anker SOLIX F3800 and EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 can produce 120/240V from a single unit, which is why they are the only batteries on this list. Some smaller stations can be paired to create 240V with a hub, and our <a href="/best-home-battery-backup/">home battery backup guide</a> covers whole-home systems that handle this natively.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wiring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 2-Wire vs 3-Wire Pumps and Why It Changes the Surge
          </h2>

          <p>
            Submersible pumps come in two common configurations. A 2-wire pump, despite the name, has two power conductors plus a ground, and puts all its starting components inside the motor down in the well. There is no control box at the surface, just a pressure switch. A 3-wire pump has three conductors plus ground and uses a control box at the surface, usually in the basement or pump house, that holds the starting capacitor and relay.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical difference for generator sizing is that 2-wire pumps are often harder on generators. Their internal start circuitry tends to pull a higher, longer inrush when the motor starts, and some generator sizing tables call for more capacity for 2-wire motors than for the equivalent 3-wire motor. A 3-wire pump's control box lets the starting components be serviced or upgraded without pulling the pump, which can matter if you later add a soft starter.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can usually tell which you have by looking near the pressure tank. If there is a gray or tan box labeled with the pump maker's name and the horsepower, often wired between the pressure switch and the well, it is a 3-wire system and that box is the control box. Its label usually lists the horsepower, voltage and amps, which are exactly the numbers you need for generator sizing. No control box usually means a 2-wire pump, and you may need the installer's records for its specs.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the paperwork is lost, the well driller or pump contractor who installed it usually has records, and a licensed electrician can measure the pump's running current with a clamp meter. Measuring starting current requires a meter that captures inrush, but it is rarely necessary: the horsepower and voltage from the control box, matched to the pump maker's sizing table, is enough to pick a generator.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="soft-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Soft Starters and Constant-Pressure Drives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The starting surge is the reason well pumps need oversized generators, so reducing it lets you use a smaller generator or make a battery system viable. There are two main ways to do that. Soft starters ramp the voltage to the motor over a moment instead of slamming it on, which lowers the inrush. Variable-speed constant-pressure drives, sold by pump makers for both new installations and retrofits, bring the motor up gently and then vary its speed to hold steady pressure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some pump lines include soft start built into the motor electronics, and constant-pressure drives from the major submersible pump makers can substantially reduce starting demand, which also tends to make them friendlier to generators and power stations. The catch is compatibility: a soft starter or drive has to be matched to the motor type, voltage and horsepower, and not every pump can take an add-on. This is a job for a pump contractor, not a weekend project.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WORTH ASKING YOUR PUMP PRO</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If you are replacing a pump anyway, ask about a soft-start or constant-pressure option.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It can lower the generator size you need, smooth out pressure in the house and reduce wear on the motor. If the pump is fine, ask whether a compatible soft starter can be added at the control box.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            A soft starter is not a substitute for a correctly sized generator, though. It lowers the surge; it does not change the running watts, and a generator that is barely adequate on paper is still a poor match. Think of soft start as the tool that makes a battery system or a quiet inverter generator workable with a 1 HP pump, or that lets a mid-size generator start the pump while the refrigerator is also running.
          </p>

          <p>
            For homeowners considering a battery system, soft start matters even more. Power stations have hard surge limits enforced by their electronics, rather than the gradual voltage sag of a generator engine, so if a pump's inrush exceeds the battery's surge rating, the inverter may simply shut off. Matching the soft-started surge to the battery's published surge specification is the step that makes a <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">power station for home backup</a> work with a well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transfer-switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Transfer Switch, Interlock Kit and Safe Connection
          </h2>

          <p>
            A well pump is hardwired, so you cannot plug it into a generator with an extension cord. The safe ways to connect it are a manual transfer switch, which moves selected circuits between grid and generator, or an interlock kit on the main panel, which physically prevents the main breaker and the generator breaker from being on at the same time. Either one is fed from a power inlet on the outside of the house, which accepts the generator's cord.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both have to be installed by a licensed electrician, usually with a permit and inspection. The well pump's double-pole breaker must be one of the circuits the transfer switch controls, and the switch and inlet must be rated for the generator's output, typically 30 amps for an L14-30 connection or 50 amps for a 14-50. Your electrician will also choose cord gauge and length so voltage drop does not make pump starts harder.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER BACKFEED</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A double-male cord plugged into a dryer outlet can kill a utility worker.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Backfeeding sends generator power out through your meter onto the utility lines, where it can be stepped up to thousands of volts. It also bypasses breakers that protect your wiring. A transfer switch or interlock is the only safe connection.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Placement and carbon monoxide safety are the other half. Every gas generator on this list produces carbon monoxide, which is invisible and can kill in minutes. Run the generator outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents, never in a garage, crawlspace or pump house. Install battery-powered CO alarms on every level of the home. Built-in CO shutoffs, which some models have, are a backstop, not permission to run closer.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want water automatically and without dragging cords in a storm, a permanent standby generator with an automatic transfer switch is the alternative. It costs much more installed, and our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">standby generator cost guide</a> covers what to expect, but for a household that loses power often and relies on a well, it can be worth it. Many people start with a portable generator and an interlock and upgrade later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pressure-tank" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Pressure Tank: Fewer Starts, Easier on the Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pressure tank is the part of a well system that decides how often the pump starts, and every start is a surge your generator has to absorb. The tank holds a cushion of pressurized water, so small draws such as a glass of water or a hand wash come from the tank instead of starting the pump. A larger tank, or a tank with more drawdown, means fewer, longer pump runs and fewer surges.
          </p>

          <p>
            A tank that has lost its air charge, often called waterlogged, makes the pump short-cycle: it turns on and off every few seconds whenever a tap runs. On grid power that wears out the pump and the pressure switch; on a generator it means a constant stream of starting surges, flickering lights, and possibly a tripped generator breaker. If you notice the pump clicking on and off rapidly, have the tank checked before an outage, not during one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Checking the tank's air precharge is a simple maintenance task: with the pump off and the tank drained, the precharge is commonly set a couple of psi below the pressure switch's cut-in pressure. Your pump contractor or the tank manual gives the exact figure. A healthy tank is one of the cheapest ways to make any generator or battery backup more reliable with a well.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tank also shows why battery backup works better for wells than people expect. A typical household might run its pump for well under an hour a day in total. Even a 1 HP pump drawing around 2,000 watts for half an hour uses only about a kilowatt-hour. Energy is rarely the problem; surge and voltage are. Once a battery can start the pump, the refrigerator and freezer usually decide how long it lasts.
          </p>
        </div>

