          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Generator for Your Well Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a typical 1/2 to 1 HP, 240V submersible pump, buy the Westinghouse WGen7500DF. It has the starting watts, the L14-30R outlet and dual fuel flexibility, and it leaves room for the kitchen and lights. If the budget is tighter, the Champion 9000-Watt Dual Fuel is the value alternative; if you want a gas-only unit with a big service network, the Generac GP8000E has a little more starting headroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a 1.5 HP pump, a deep well with a 1 HP pump on a 2-wire motor, or a household that wants the well and most of the house on one generator, buy the DuroMax XP13000EH. Its extra surge margin is what makes pump starts reliable while other motors are running. If noise is the priority and your pump is 3/4 HP or smaller, the Honda EU7000iS is the quiet choice that still produces 240 volts.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want silent, fuel-free backup, choose the Anker SOLIX F3800 for its 6,000-watt output and headroom, or the EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 for 1/2 to 3/4 HP pumps. Check each one's surge rating against your pump maker's table, and ask your pump contractor about soft start if the numbers are close.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, the generator is only half the purchase. Budget for a licensed electrician to install an inlet and a transfer switch or interlock that includes the well pump circuit, a properly rated cord, CO alarms in the house, and a pressure tank check. Then test the whole setup on a calm day before the storm, with the pump starting under generator power, so the first real outage is uneventful.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 120V-only generator for a 240V pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many quiet inverter and small portable generators produce only 120 volts, and no amount of wattage will run a 240V submersible pump from them. Check your panel for a double-pole pump breaker, which signals 240V, then buy a generator with a 120/240V L14-30R or 14-50R outlet and confirm the transfer equipment matches it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing from running watts instead of starting watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1 HP pump may run on around 2,000 watts but need two to three times that to start. A generator sized for running watts will bog down, trip its breaker or stall the motor. Match your pump maker's generator sizing table, and add margin for other motors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding through a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-male cord into a dryer or range outlet sends generator power onto the utility lines, endangering line workers and bypassing your breakers. It is illegal in most places. Have a licensed electrician install an inlet with a transfer switch or interlock kit that includes the pump circuit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a waterlogged pressure tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tank that has lost its air charge makes the pump short-cycle every time a tap runs, which means a constant stream of starting surges on the generator. Have the tank's precharge checked and the bladder inspected before storm season, when it is a small repair rather than an emergency.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the generator too close to the house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Generators produce carbon monoxide that can build up indoors quickly. Never run one in a garage, pump house, crawlspace or near windows. Keep it at least 20 feet away with the exhaust pointed away, and install battery CO alarms on every level of the home.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

