        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts does a well pump use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Running watts depend on horsepower: roughly 1,000 to 1,200 watts for a 1/2 HP pump, 1,400 to 1,600 for 3/4 HP, 1,800 to 2,200 for 1 HP and 2,400 to 3,000 for 1.5 HP. Starting watts are typically two to three times higher. These are planning ranges; your pump's nameplate amps and its maker's sizing table give the real figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need for a 1 HP well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical 1 HP, 240V submersible pump, plan on a generator in the 7,500-to-10,000-watt class with a 120/240V outlet, so the pump can start with other loads on. Pump makers' sizing tables often call for several kilowatts of generator capacity for a 1 HP motor alone. Larger margins help with 2-wire pumps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many amps does a well pump draw?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At 240 volts, a 1/2 HP pump typically draws around 4 to 6 amps running, a 3/4 HP pump around 6 to 8, a 1 HP pump around 8 to 10 and a 1.5 HP pump around 10 to 13. Starting current is several times higher for a moment. The control box or motor nameplate lists the exact full-load amps for your pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an inverter generator run a well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it produces the right voltage and enough starting watts. Most small inverter generators are 120V only and cannot run a 240V pump. Larger 120/240V inverter models such as the Honda EU7000iS can run a 1/2 to 3/4 HP pump comfortably and a 1 HP pump started on its own, with a transfer switch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run a well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A few can. It needs native 240V output for most submersible pumps and enough surge capacity for the pump's start. The Anker SOLIX F3800 and EcoFlow Delta Pro 3 produce 120/240V. Check their published surge ratings against your pump maker's table, and consider soft start for 1 HP and larger pumps. Most smaller stations cannot do this job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a transfer switch for a well pump generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, or an interlock kit with a power inlet. A well pump is hardwired on a 240V double-pole circuit, so the only safe way to power it from a generator is through transfer equipment installed by a licensed electrician. Never backfeed through a dryer outlet with a double-male cord, which can electrocute utility workers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a 2-wire and 3-wire well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 2-wire pump has its starting components inside the motor in the well, with no surface control box. A 3-wire pump uses a control box near the pressure tank that holds the start capacitor and relay. 2-wire pumps often have a harsher starting surge, so generator sizing tables may call for a larger generator for them.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
